      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπειδὴ ὀδυνώμενος ἂν διαπεφωνήκειν",
          "english_quote": "since I would have cried out in pain.",
          "issue": "διαπεφωνήκειν is not adequately rendered as an outcry here. διαφωνέω has a sense of failing or perishing, and Peter's immediately following question, διὰ τοῦτο τεθνήξει, confirms that Clement has spoken of dying. The draft substitutes crying out for that claim, and its lexical note reinforces the substitution.",
          "suggested_revision": "Submit 'since I would have died in anguish' for fresh review. Rewrite the lexical note to explain the contextual sense of διαπεφωνήκειν and the unreal result expressed by the pluperfect with ἄν.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "12.5",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἠγωνίων μήπως ἀπολειφθείς σου, ἄνθρωπος ὢν νεώτερος, ὅπερ νῦν οὕτως ἐνστάσεως ἔχω, κἂν κατὰ τινὰ χόλον θεοῦ ἀποστῆναί σου ἀδύνατον εἰ ἥττων ἐπιθυμίας ἔσομαι",
          "english_quote": "I was anxious that, if separated from you, young man that I am, I might prove weaker than desire. Yet now I have such resistance in this matter that, even if I were to leave you because of some wrath from God, it would be impossible for me to prove weaker than desire.",
          "issue": "The single expression εἰ ἥττων ἐπιθυμίας ἔσομαι effectively supplies two English assertions: the outcome Clement feared and an outcome he now declares impossible. The earlier μήπως construction contains no separate expression of becoming weaker than desire. The note discloses competing attachments, but does not establish that the final clause can both complete the earlier anxiety and independently express immunity to desire. Moreover, the supplied infinitive ἀποστῆναί σου directly precedes ἀδύνατον. The problem is the unsupported completion and doubled assertion, not merely the existence of alternative interpretations.",
          "suggested_revision": "Develop one coherent construction without using the final εἰ-clause to complete both readings. A candidate for fresh review would take εἰ ἥττων ἐπιθυμίας ἔσομαι as resuming Clement's anxiety—'whether I would be weaker than desire'—and consider ἀποστῆναί σου ἀδύνατον as 'it is impossible to leave you.' Explain the attachments of ὅπερ, ἐνστάσεως, and κἄν, preserving viable alternatives in notes. If retaining the result interpretation instead, demonstrate how the earlier μήπως construction is represented without inventing a second outcome. No alteration of the verified Greek is warranted by this finding.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
